@ id: lbq03
@ order: 603
@ title: Why QAT, not PTQ — the weights must LEARN to be ternary
@ subtitle: post-training quantization collapses at 1.58 bits; training-in-the-loop does not
@ source: arxiv 2310.11453

--- note
## Two ways to get a low-bit model
There are two fundamentally different routes to a low-precision LLM, and the distinction is the whole
reason BitNet exists:

- **Post-training quantization (PTQ).** Take a **finished, full-precision** model and **round** its
  weights (and maybe activations) down to low precision *after* training. Simple, cheap, no retraining
  — GPTQ, SmoothQuant, AWQ, QuIP, Absmax all live here. The problem: the model "is **not optimized for
  the quantized representation** during training," so quality degrades, and the loss gets **worse the
  lower you go**.
- **Quantization-aware training (QAT).** **Simulate** the low-precision rounding **during training**
  (via the STE, lbq02), so the weights **learn to be robust to it**. More expensive, but "typically
  results in better accuracy, as the model is trained to account for the reduced precision from the
  beginning." BitNet is QAT taken to the extreme case — **1-bit / 1.58-bit**.

--- note
## The evidence: PTQ collapses as bits → 1, QAT does not
The original BitNet paper (2310.11453) puts the two approaches on the same axes. As you push PTQ methods
toward very low bit-widths their accuracy **falls off a cliff toward random**, and perplexity **explodes**
— e.g. Absmax PTQ at 1-bit reaches a perplexity of **3.5×10²³** (i.e. a broken model). BitNet, which is
**trained** at 1-bit from scratch (the red star), stays up near the full-precision line where every PTQ
curve has already collapsed. This is the picture that motivates the entire line of work.

--- image
