Figure 1 (2310.11453), four panels. TOP-LEFT: BitNet is "Training from Scratch" (trained W-bits = inference W-bits, on the diagonal) whereas PTQ methods (FP8, SmoothQuant, GPTQ, QuIP) train at 16-bit and only *quantize* for inference (bottom row). TOP-RIGHT: **Accuracy vs Weight Bits** — the SoTA PTQ curve degrades steeply from 8→1 bit and heads toward "Random", while BitNet (★, trained from scratch) holds high accuracy at 1 bit. BOTTOM-LEFT: energy-cost reduction grows with model size (8.8×→38.8×). BOTTOM-RIGHT: BitNet's loss curve runs **parallel** to the FP16 Transformer's, with a small, roughly constant gap **ΔL = 0.09** that shrinks as models scale.

--- note
## Why PTQ *cannot* reach 1.58 bits and QAT can
Rounding a trained weight to the nearest ternary value introduces an error. In a full-precision model the
weights were placed assuming they'd be used at full precision — so **rounding to {-1,0,+1} is a large,
uncorrected perturbation**, and there is no gradient step left to compensate. Under QAT the opposite is
true: because the forward pass **already used the ternary weights** at every step, the loss the optimizer
minimized **already includes the rounding error**, and the STE let it adjust the latent weights so the
ternary pattern is a *good* one. The network effectively **learns a weight distribution that quantizes
well** — clustering values near -1, 0, +1 — rather than being forced onto a grid it was never trained
for. That is why b1.58 can **match FP16** at 3B+ while PTQ to the same precision collapses.

--- note
## This is exactly the Bonsai-debunk distinction (our own finding)
Our `docs/bonsai_ternary_lowbit_findings.md` audited a "ternary Bonsai" project claimed to be low-bit
*training*. The verdict: Bonsai is **PTQ of off-the-shelf Qwen3 checkpoints** into a group-wise ternary
*format* + custom kernels — a **deployment/inference demo**, with **no QAT, no STE, no from-scratch
low-bit training**. The paper we are reading here is precisely what Bonsai is *not*: it never discloses a
rounding rule that keeps ~95% quality at 1.58 bits, because "pure round-to-nearest PTQ to 1.58 bits
normally collapses quality; real BitNet b1.58 needed **QAT from scratch**." The genuinely reusable,
reproducible primitive — ternary {-1,0,+1} + absmean scaling + STE training — comes from **BitNet b1.58**,
and that is what we built into the `lowbit-qat` agent. (Contrast with **g405**, Gemma 4's *post-hoc* QAT
of already-trained checkpoints: a milder, format-oriented QAT that still starts from a full model — see
lbq05.)
